The '''2025 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2024–25 UEFA Champions League]], the 70th season of Europe's premier club football tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 33rd season since it was renamed from the [[European Champion Clubs' Cup]] to the [[UEFA Champions League]]. It was held at the [[Allianz Arena]] in [[Munich]], Germany, on 31 May 2025, between French club [[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]] and Italian club [[Inter Milan]]. This was Inter’s second [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League finals|Champions League Final]] in three years, the last being in [[2023 UEFA Champions League final|2023]], where they lost to [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] 1-0. This was PSG’s first Champions League Final since [[2020 UEFA Champions League final|2020]], where they also lost 1-0, but to [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]]. 

Paris Saint-Germain won the match 5–0 for their first European Cup title,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0299-1de417608530-15b01ff7b150-1000/ |title=Paris win Champions League: Doué double helps secure record-breaking final win against Inter |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=31 May 2025 |access-date=31 May 2025}}</ref> marking the second final victory by a French club since [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] in [[1993 UEFA Champions League final|1993]]. Their margin of victory was the largest in any of the main European men's club competitions{{efn|name=major}} final, with their goal tally only surpassed in one [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League finals|European Cup final]] by [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]]'s seven in [[1960 European Cup final|1960]]. In doing so, Paris Saint-Germain completed a [[Treble (association football)#Continental trebles|continental treble]], the first of a French club, while [[Luis Enrique]] became the second manager since [[Pep Guardiola]] to have won the treble twice. As winners, Paris Saint-Germain earned the right to play against [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]], winners of the [[2024–25 UEFA Europa League]], in the [[2025 UEFA Super Cup]], and automatically qualified for the [[2025 FIFA Intercontinental Cup final]] and the [[2029 FIFA Club World Cup]]. The match would also be [[Simone Inzaghi]]'s final match as manager of Inter Milan as he would leave his post three days after the final by mutual consent.

==Background==
Paris Saint-Germain reached their second Champions League final and the first since [[2020 UEFA Champions League final|2020]], when they lost to [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=23 August 2020 |title=Triple perfekt! Coman köpft Bayern auf Europas Thron |trans-title=Treble perfect! Coman heads Bayern to the European throne |url=https://www.kicker.de/paris-sg-gegen-bayern-2020-champions-league-4651902/analyse |access-date=23 August 2020 |work=[[Kicker (sports magazine)|kicker]]}}</ref> This was the fifth European final for them, having previously also appeared in two consecutive finals of the [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup]], winning the [[1996 UEFA Cup Winners' Cup final|1996 final]] 1–0 against [[SK Rapid Wien|Rapid Wien]] before losing by the same scoreline against [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] while attempting to defend their title in [[1997 UEFA Cup Winners' Cup final|1997]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=30 June 2005 |title=UEFA Cup Winners' Cup: All-time finals |url=http://en.archive.uefa.com/uefa/news/kind=1/newsid=2577.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120716101258/http://en.archive.uefa.com/uefa/news/kind=1/newsid=2577.html |archive-date=16 July 2012|access-date=1 June 2025|website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations|via=[[Internet Archive]]|url-status=dead}}</ref> The club also contested the [[1996 UEFA Super Cup]], losing 9–2 on aggregate to [[Juventus FC|Juventus]]. Paris Saint-Germain attempted to become only the second French team to win the European Cup/Champions League, following the success of [[Le Classique|their rivals]] [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] in [[1993 UEFA Champions League final|1993]]—with the final also taking place in Munich—and the first French club to complete a [[Treble (association football)#Continental trebles|continental treble]], having won the [[2024–25 Ligue 1|Ligue 1]] and the [[2024–25 Coupe de France|Coupe de France]].<ref name="treble">{{Cite web |title=Paris complete the 11th domestic league, domestic cup and European Cup/UEFA Champions League treble |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0251-0e99b1ba85da-ec8053dc0a29-1000--paris-complete-the-11th-domestic-league-domestic-cup-and-e/ |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=31 May 2025 |access-date=31 May 2025 }}</ref> The club had won the [[2024 Trophée des Champions|Trophée des Champions]] (super cup) earlier in the season, thus had a chance for a clean sweep of titles available to them.<ref>{{cite web|date=5 January 2025|title=Paris beat Monaco to lift Trophée des Champions|url=https://en.psg.fr/teams/first-team/content/paris-beat-monaco-to-lift-trophee-des-champions-compte-rendu-paris-saint-germain-as-monaco-ligue-1-2024-2025|accessdate=5 January 2025|publisher=Paris Saint-Germain|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250105192246/https://en.psg.fr/teams/first-team/content/paris-beat-monaco-to-lift-trophee-des-champions-compte-rendu-paris-saint-germain-as-monaco-ligue-1-2024-2025|archive-date=5 January 2025|url-status=dead}}</ref> Their manager, [[Luis Enrique]], was aiming for a second continental treble, having done so with Barcelona in the [[2014–15 FC Barcelona season|2014–15 season]]. Since he was successful, he became the second manager to achieve such a feat after fellow former Barcelona player and manager [[Pep Guardiola]], who achieved so in [[2008–09 FC Barcelona season|2008–09]] with Barcelona and in [[2022–23 Manchester City F.C. season|2022–23]] with [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]].<ref name="treble"/> He also became the seventh [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League winning managers|manager to win the European Cup/Champions League]] with different clubs.<ref>{{Cite web |date=31 May 2025 |title=Paris Saint-Germain vs. Inter score: PSG crowned Champions League winners for first time with blowout victory|first1=James|last1=Benge|first2=Chuck |last2=Booth|url=https://www.cbssports.com/soccer/news/paris-saint-germain-vs-inter-score-psg-crowned-champions-league-winners-for-first-time-with-blowout-victory/live/ |access-date=31 May 2025|website=CBS Sports}}</ref>

This was Inter Milan's thirteenth European final and seventh European Cup/Champions League final appearance, having won 3–1 in [[1964 European Cup final|1964]] against [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], 1–0 against [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] at [[San Siro|home]] in [[1965 European Cup final|1965]] and 2–0 against Bayern Munich in [[2010 UEFA Champions League final|2010]], resulting in Inter completing the continental treble; they also lost 2–1 to [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] in [[1967 European Cup final|1967]], 2–0 to [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] in [[1972 European Cup final|1972]] and 1–0 to Manchester City in [[2023 UEFA Champions League final|2023]],<ref name="RSSSF"/> all of which enabled those clubs to win the continental treble themselves.<ref name="treble"/> The club had also contested five UEFA Cup/UEFA Europa League finals, winning in [[1991 UEFA Cup final|1991]], [[1994 UEFA Cup final|1994]], and [[1998 UEFA Cup final|1998]] as well as losing in [[1997 UEFA Cup final|1997]] and [[2020 UEFA Europa League final|2020]].<ref name="UC">{{Cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |title=UEFA Cup |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec3b.html |access-date=2 June 2025 |publisher=[[RSSSF]]}}</ref> They also contested the [[2010 UEFA Super Cup]], losing 2–0 to [[Atlético Madrid]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |title=European Super Cup |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tabless/sup.html |access-date=2 June 2025 |publisher=[[RSSSF]]}}</ref> Inter Milan were the most recent Italian club to win the Champions League; since 2010 only one other Italian club—[[Derby d'Italia|their rivals]] Juventus—had reached the Champions League final, losing in [[2015 UEFA Champions League final|2015]] and [[2017 UEFA Champions League final|2017]].<ref name="RSSSF"/> [[Simone Inzaghi]] was aiming for his first European title as manager and the second in his football career, having won against [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in the [[1999 UEFA Super Cup]] as a [[SS Lazio|Lazio]] player.<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 June 2021 |title=Inter Milan hire ex-Lazio boss Simone Inzaghi as new coach |access-date=6 June 2025 |website=ESPN |url=https://www.espn.com/football/story/_/id/37617682/inter-milan-hire-ex-lazio-boss-simone-inzaghi-new-coach }}</ref> Their midfielder [[Henrikh Mkhitaryan]] was aiming to become the eleventh player to have [[UEFA club competition records and statistics#List of players to have won the three main European club competitions|won all three major European trophies]] and the first to have achieved that with three different clubs, having won the [[2016–17 UEFA Europa League]] with Manchester United and the [[2021–22 UEFA Europa Conference League]] with [[AS Roma|Roma]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=29 May 2025 |title=What players have won all three UEFA tournaments? Henrikh Mkhitaryan on verge of historic Champions League title |first=Kyle |last=Bonn |access-date=6 June 2025 |website=[[The Sporting News]] |url=https://www.sportingnews.com/uk/football/news/players-won-all-three-uefa-tournaments-champions-league/7ea81117685932ba4a3fede3 }}</ref>

The clubs had never met in any competitive matches, but had faced each other in five exhibition games.<ref>{{cite web |title=PSG vs. Inter: the Champions League final is set |url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/champions-league-final-24-25-inter-psg |website=Inter.it |publisher=Inter Milan |access-date=10 May 2025 |date=8 May 2025}}</ref>

The match was the first Champions League final without a club from England, Spain or Germany since Portuguese side [[FC Porto|Porto]] defeated French representative [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]] in [[2004 UEFA Champions League final|2004]].<ref name="RSSSF"/>

