==Venue==
{{Further|Allianz Arena}}
[[File:Allianz Arena (Bayern Munich).jpg|thumb|upright=1.2|The [[Allianz Arena]] in [[Munich]] hosted the final.]]

This was the second UEFA Champions League final hosted at the [[Allianz Arena]] in [[Munich]]; the first was held in [[2012 UEFA Champions League final|2012]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0283-186742eb3c74-d5eb4afca229-1000--2025-uefa-champions-league-final-munich-football-arena/ |title=2025 UEFA Champions League final: Munich Football Arena |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=12 March 2025 |access-date=18 March 2025}}</ref> Overall, it was the fifth [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League finals|European Cup final]] to be held in Munich, with the [[1979 European Cup final|1979]], [[1993 UEFA Champions League final|1993]], [[1997 UEFA Champions League final|1997]], and [[2012 UEFA Champions League final|2012]] finals taking place at the [[Olympiastadion (Munich)|Olympiastadion]]. Each of the previous four finals played in Munich saw a first-time winner of the competition crowned ([[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]] in 1979, [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] in 1993, [[Borussia Dortmund]] in 1997, and [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in 2012). The final also was the ninth to take place in Germany, having also taken place in [[Stuttgart]] in [[1959 European Cup final|1959]] and [[1988 European Cup final|1988]], [[Gelsenkirchen]] in [[2004 UEFA Champions League final|2004]] and [[Berlin]] in [[2015 UEFA Champions League final|2015]], equalling the record of nine European Cup finals held in Italy and England.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/EuroExperience/competitions/Publications/01/99/48/32/1994832_DOWNLOAD.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140712134245/http://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/EuroExperience/competitions/Publications/01/99/48/32/1994832_DOWNLOAD.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=12 July 2014 |title=UEFA Champions League Statistics Handbook 2013/14: Finals |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |year=2014 |access-date=24 September 2019}}</ref> The Allianz Arena previously hosted matches at the [[2006 FIFA World Cup]],<ref name="StadiaNames">{{cite news |date=6 June 2006 |title=Stadiums renamed for Fifa sponsors |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/business/4773843.stm |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180613190159/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/business/4773843.stm |archive-date=13 June 2018 |access-date=29 March 2008 |publisher=BBC}}</ref> and was chosen as a host venue for [[UEFA Euro 2020]] and [[UEFA Euro 2024]].

===Host selection===
On 16 July 2021, the UEFA Executive Committee announced that the [[Atatürk Olympic Stadium]] in [[Istanbul]] would host the [[2023 UEFA Champions League final]] instead of Munich.<ref name="Munich 2025">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/026b-12c542441355-1c2040c5e54b-1000/ |title=Venues appointed for club competition finals |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=16 July 2021 |access-date=16 July 2021}}</ref> This was because Istanbul twice had the Champions League final intended for their city relocated due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]]. Originally planned as hosts for the [[2020 UEFA Champions League final|2020 final]], the match was moved to [[Lisbon]] and the final hosts shifted back a year, with Istanbul instead awarded the [[2021 UEFA Champions League final|2021 final]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025e-0fb6101cfdbf-8b878372d79d-1000--uefa-competitions-to-resume-in-august/ |title=UEFA competitions to resume in August |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 June 2020 |access-date=17 June 2020}}</ref> However, weeks prior to the final, the 2021 fixture was moved to [[Porto]] due to travel restrictions.<ref name="Porto">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0269-1243f864391e-53c7874f0ae0-1000/ |title=UEFA Champions League final to move to Portugal to allow 6,000 fans of each team to attend |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=13 May 2021 |access-date=13 May 2021 |archive-date=13 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210513105402/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/news/0269-1243f864391e-53c7874f0ae0-1000/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Munich, originally selected to host the [[2022 UEFA Champions League final|2022 final]] by the UEFA Executive Committee during their meeting in [[Ljubljana]], Slovenia on 24 September 2019,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0255-0e99f55f1d05-6273cba59e7c-1000--champions-league-final-hosts-for-2021-2022-2023-and-2024/ |title=Champions League final hosts announced for 2021, 2022 and 2023 |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=24 September 2019 |access-date=24 September 2019}}</ref> was later planned to host the [[2023 UEFA Champions League final|2023 final]] after the shifting of the final hosts. However, the city was awarded the 2025 final instead after being bumped from 2023 by Istanbul.<ref name="Munich 2025"/>

