==Post-match==
[[File:2015 UEFA Super Cup 66.jpg|thumb|Paris Saint-Germain's [[Luis Enrique]] won both his second Champions League title and continental treble as manager.]]

With their victory, Paris Saint-Germain won their first Champions league title, the 24th team to do so. It also marked the second final to be won by a French club, after [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] in [[1993 UEFA Champions League final|1993]].<ref name="RSSSF">{{cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec1.html |title=European Champions' Cup/Champions League |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |access-date=2 June 2025}}</ref> Paris Saint-Germain also completed the eleventh [[Treble (association football)#Continental trebles|continental treble]] in European football history, doing so as the ninth overall team and first French club.<ref name="treble"/> It was the [[List of association football teams to have won four or more trophies in one season#Four titles in one season|fourth trophy of the season]] for Paris Saint-Germain, having also won the [[2024 Trophée des Champions|Trophée des Champions]]. [[Luis Enrique]] won his second Champions League title as manager, having previously won with [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] in [[2015 UEFA Champions League final|2015]] as part of a treble. With the victory, he became the 21st coach to win multiple finals and the seventh to do so with multiple clubs.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0275-15407684c988-5b733e851bf5-1000/ |title=Champions League-winning coaches: Carlo Ancelotti leads the way, Luis Enrique becomes seventh to triumph with two clubs |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=31 May 2025 |access-date=2 June 2025}}</ref> Additionally, he became the second manager to win multiple trebles after fellow Spaniard [[Pep Guardiola]], who also won his first treble with Barcelona.<ref name="treble"/>

Paris Saint-Germain's five unanswered goals set a record for the largest margin of victory in the final of any of the main European men's club competitions;{{efn|name=major|The main European men's club competitions also includes the [[List of UEFA Cup Winners' Cup finals|Cup Winners' Cup]] (defunct), [[List of UEFA Cup and Europa League finals|UEFA Cup/Europa League]] and [[List of UEFA Conference League finals|(Europa) Conference League]].<ref name="RSSSF"/><ref>{{cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec2.html |title=European Cup Winners' Cup |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |date=26 January 2000 |access-date=6 June 2025}}</ref><ref name="UC"/><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaconferenceleague/news/0298-1d6e887134f4-450771540b4a-1000/ |title=UEFA Conference League winners, best players, goals and teams of the season |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=1 June 2025 |access-date=6 June 2025}}</ref>}} for a European Cup final, this result surpassed the four-goal differences in [[1960 European Cup final|1960]], [[1974 European Cup final#Replay|1974 replay]], [[1989 European Cup final|1989]] and [[1994 UEFA Champions League final|1994]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0252-0e98c715bb79-dea77f56e2bd-1000/ |title=Champions League final records and statistics |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=31 May 2025 |access-date=2 June 2025}}</ref> It also equaled, with [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] in [[1962 European Cup final|1962]], for the second-most goals scored by a team in the final, only behind the seven of [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] in 1960.<ref name="RSSSF"/> [[Senny Mayulu]] became the second-youngest scorer in a Champions League final, only behind [[Patrick Kluivert]] in [[1995 UEFA Champions League final|1995]], while [[Désiré Doué]] became the fourth-youngest.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://sportstar.thehindu.com/football/champions-league/who-is-senny-mayulu-second-youngest-player-to-score-champions-league-final-psg-vs-inter-ucl-latest-news-updates/article69643130.ece |title=Who is Senny Mayulu, the second-youngest player ever to score in a Champions League final? |work=[[Sportstar]] |date=1 June 2025 |access-date=2 June 2025}}</ref> Doué was named as the [[man of the match]] for his brace,<ref name="MOTM"/> and was subsequently selected as the Champions League Young Player of the Season.<ref>{{cite news|date=1 June 2025|title=Désiré Doué named 2024/25 UEFA Champions League Young Player of the Season|access-date=1 June 2025|website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/029a-1de593e81763-a1312db28c5a-1000}}</ref> The win for Paris Saint-Germain also meant for the first time since [[FC Porto|Porto]]'s win in the [[2003–04 UEFA Champions League|2003–04 season]] that a club from outside of Europe's four big leagues (England, Spain, Italy, and Germany) had won the tournament. 

Inter Milan's loss was their fourth in a Champions League final (after [[1967 European Cup final|1967]], [[1972 European Cup final|1972]], and [[2023 UEFA Champions League final|2023]]) and the second in three seasons.<ref name="RSSSF"/> Having finished second in [[2024–25 Serie A|Serie A]] and the [[2024 Supercoppa Italiana|Supercoppa Italiana]] and exited the [[2024–25 Coppa Italia|Coppa Italia]] in the semi-finals, the defeat meant Inter would finish a season without a trophy for the first time since the [[2019–20 Inter Milan season|2019–20 season]], when they also lost a European final. Inter manager [[Simone Inzaghi]] called the defeat "painful" and "disappointing" but said Inter would bounce back from the loss like they did in 2023.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/inzaghi-interview-psg-inter-champions-league-final |title=Inzaghi: "It hurts, but PSG were the better team. I've thanked the lads" |publisher=Inter Milan |date=1 June 2025 |access-date=3 June 2025}}</ref> However, the final turned out to be Inzaghi's last game in charge of Inter as he left the club by mutual consent three days later.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.inter.it/en/news/simone-inzaghi-message-inter |title=Simone Inzaghi's message |publisher=Inter Milan |date=3 June 2025 |access-date=3 June 2025}}</ref>

As winners, Paris Saint-Germain qualified for various other competitions. They competed in the [[2025 UEFA Super Cup]] against [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]], winners of the [[2024–25 UEFA Europa League]]. They also qualified for the [[2025 FIFA Intercontinental Cup final|final]] of the [[2025 FIFA Intercontinental Cup]], where they competed against [[CR Flamengo|Flamengo]], another continental champion, for the title of [[List of world champion football clubs|annual club world champions]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Lopez Vega |first=Alejandro |url=https://bolavip.com/en/soccer/luis-enrique-leads-psg-to-champions-league-ligue-1-and-french-cup-titles-which-other-coaches-have-won-the-treble |title=Luis Enrique leads PSG to Champions League, Ligue 1 and French Cup titles: Which other coaches have won the treble? |work=Bolavip |date=31 May 2025 |access-date=31 May 2025}}</ref> Finally, they qualified for the [[2029 FIFA Club World Cup]], FIFA's quadrennial club championship.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/club-world-cup/usa-2025/articles/champions-league-final-review-2025 |title=Doue fires PSG to Champions League glory |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=31 May 2025 |access-date=2 June 2025}}</ref>

===Celebrations and riots===
{{Main|2025 Paris Saint-Germain celebration riots}}

Following Paris Saint-Germain's victory, celebrations were held across France overnight. In some areas, [[2025 Paris Saint-Germain celebration riots|disorder broke out]] and across the country two people were killed and a further 192 were injured, with 559 people being arrested. A 17-year-old boy died after being stabbed in [[Dax, Landes|Dax]] and a 23-year-old man was killed in central [[Paris]] after being hit by a vehicle whilst riding a scooter. The [[Minister of the Interior (France)|interior ministry]] said that 491 people were arrested in Paris alone and 264 vehicles were set on fire; among the injured were 22 police officers and seven firefighters. Four people were injured in [[Grenoble]] after a car ploughed into PSG fans. During the celebrations shops were looted, bus shelters smashed and flares and fireworks set off, with clashes breaking out near the [[Champs-Élysées]] and the [[Parc des Princes]]. Around 5,400 police were deployed across Paris in anticipation of disorder during the celebrations, and [[Paris Police Prefecture|Paris police prefect]] said the trophy parade would go ahead the next day but with a higher police and military presence.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mackintosh |first1=Thomas |last2=Lamche |first2=Anna |title=Two dead and hundreds arrested in France after PSG Champions League win |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ckgqyg325gno |access-date=1 June 2025 |work=[[BBC News]] |date=1 June 2025}}</ref>

